WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.555
Public key infrastructure is the trust framework that makes web service authentication

00:00:04.555 --> 00:00:04.940
scale.

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:08.900
Server certificates, certificate authorities, intermediate chains, trust stores,

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:12.180
revocation mechanisms, certificate transparency practices,

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:16.440
and browser or operating system policies all contribute to the decision a client makes

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:18.155
when it accepts a TLS connection.

00:00:18.155 --> 00:00:21.320
For a web service, the certificate is not just a file on disk;

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:23.405
it is part of a larger ecosystem of trust.

00:00:23.405 --> 00:00:26.070
Digital signatures are central to that ecosystem.

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:28.700
Certificates are signed, certificate chains are verified,

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:31.580
and clients must understand the signature algorithms involved.

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:35.620
Post-quantum signature algorithms can introduce larger signatures or certificates,

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:40.080
and that may affect handshake size, network behavior, memory usage, embedded clients,

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:42.300
legacy devices, middleboxes, and logging.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:44.640
The concern is not only whether an algorithm is strong;

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.660
it is whether the surrounding ecosystem can use it reliably.

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:49.965
Certificate migration depends on many parties.

00:00:49.965 --> 00:00:53.765
Browsers, operating systems, certificate authorities, TLS libraries,

00:00:53.765 --> 00:00:58.270
hardware security modules, cloud platforms, device vendors, enterprise trust stores,

00:00:58.270 --> 00:01:02.070
mobile platforms, and security inspection products all influence readiness.

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:03.805
A service owner may be eager to move,

00:01:03.805 --> 00:01:08.230
but if clients cannot validate the chain or a managed platform does not support the needed

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:08.625
options,

00:01:08.625 --> 00:01:10.920
production migration may be blocked or risky.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:13.140
It also helps to separate related use cases.

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:16.720
Code signing, service identity, device identity,

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:19.480
and web server certificates all use digital signatures,

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:23.680
but they have different validation paths, lifetimes, and operational consequences.

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:27.680
Teams should track ecosystem readiness before changing production certificate strategy.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:32.065
The near-term planning move is to inventory certificate use, understand dependencies,

00:01:32.065 --> 00:01:33.325
follow reputable guidance,

00:01:33.325 --> 00:01:36.800
and avoid one-off certificate choices that clients cannot handle.
